<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Guandu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/200">200</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Guandu was fought in 200 CE near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between the warlords <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>. Though heavily outnumbered, Cao Cao won by raiding and burning Yuan Shao's supply depot at Wuchao. The victory made him master of northern China and laid the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-guandu.jpg" alt="Battle of Guandu" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Zhuwq on zh.wikipedia; derivative work by Deadkid dk 02:40, 10 July 2009 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guanduzhizhan_eng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> had become apparent by 196. Yuan Shao held the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> region, while Cao Cao controlled most of the land to the south after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> at the Battle of Xiapi in 199, and kept Emperor Xian with him in the new capital at Xu. Each saw the other as the obstacle to his ambition to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the autumn of 199 Cao Cao stationed troops at Guandu, which lay on the road to Xu near the Yellow River. In 200 Yuan Shao, whose army numbered up to 110,000, including 10,000 cavalry, marched to Liyang, after his adviser Tian Feng had urged him not to and been imprisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>'s general Yan Liang besieged the fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boma">Boma</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> feinted towards Yan Ford on the advice of Xun You and then struck east; Yan Liang was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>. Cao Cao then abandoned his forward bases to hold Guandu, still outnumbered. Both armies dug earthworks, and Yuan Shao's archers on high platforms were answered by Cao Cao's traction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchets</a>. When supplies ran short, Cao Cao considered retreat, but Xun Yu wrote to dissuade him, and in the ninth month raiders under Xu Huang burned grain carts at Gushi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the tenth month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>'s adviser Xu You, dissatisfied with his lord, defected and told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> that the depot at Wuchao, under Chunyu Qiong, was lightly guarded. Cao Cao led 5,000 infantry and cavalry there at night, disguised as Yuan Shao's reinforcements, and burned almost all the food supplies. Yuan Shao sent his main army under Zhang He and Gao Lan against Guandu instead, but it failed, and the two generals surrendered. Yuan Shao fled north across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> with about 800 cavalry. Cao Cao had captured men buried alive, and claimed to have killed 70,000 enemy troops.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> died in June 202, and his sons Yuan Tan and Yuan Shang fought each other, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> exploited. By 207 most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was under his control. Sima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> blamed Yuan Shao's obstinacy for the defeat, while Carl Leban credits Cao Cao's choice of ground and Rafe de Crespigny doubts that Yuan Shao held a real advantage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> cited the battle as an example of a weaker side yielding at first and then defeating a stronger one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Guandu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cao Cao, holding the last Han emperor, Emperor Xian, in his power, reunified northern China after defeating Yuan Shao at <b>Guandu</b> in 200, and became King of Wei in 216.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li></ul></section>
